The code statements that lead to the bug are:
mddev_put(mddev);
if (kn)
    sysfs_unbreak_active_protection(kn);
Reason:
The original code called mddev_put() before sysfs_unbreak_active_protection(). mddev_put() can make the mddev eligible for deletion, and then sysfs_unbreak_active_protection() drops the last kobject reference, triggering kobject teardown while the sysfs write path is still active. This causes recursive locking on kn->active because kernfs_remove operations happen inside the same sysfs store context.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (kn)
    sysfs_unbreak_active_protection(kn);
mddev_put(mddev);
Reason:
The fix restores active protection (sysfs_unbreak_active_protection) before releasing the mddev reference (mddev_put). This ensures that any extra kobject reference taken by sysfs_break_active_protection() is dropped while the mddev is still held alive. The actual md kobject deletion is then deferred until after the sysfs write path has fully unwound, preventing the recursive locking on kn->active.

